\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Finite Gaussian backgrounds and relabelings}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{To compare two discrete Gaussian models, specify which data a change
of labels must carry. A \newvocab{finite Gaussian background} consists
of a \vocabk{finite incidence presentation}{fdip-0002}
#{(V,E,s,h)}, positive weights #{w:E\to\mathbb R_{>0}}, a mass
#{m>0}, and a nonempty proper retained subset #{R\subsetneq V}.
Put #{U=V\setminus R}; both retained and eliminated carriers are
nonempty. The specified reference structures are the
\vocabk{counting inner products}{fdip-0002} on vertex and edge fields,
the \vocabk{weighted edge inner product}{fdip-0007}, and the
[induced Borel Euclidean volume](fdip-002H) #{\lambda_V} on
#{\mathbb R^V}. The [orthogonal coordinate split](fdip-002Q)
identifies it with the product of the retained and eliminated volumes.}

\p{Let #{d:\mathbb R^V\to\mathbb R^E} be incidence,
#{(df)(e)=f(h(e))-f(s(e))}, and let #{W} multiply edge fields by
#{w}. The derived operators are
##{L=d^\dagger Wd,\qquad Q=L+m^2I>0,}
where #{d^\dagger} is the [counting adjoint](fdip-001P).
In counting bases, #{d} has array #{D} and #{L=D^TWD}.
The [action](fdip-0004) #{\mathcal A_Q(f)=b_V(f,Qf)/2}, finite
positive [normalizer](fdip-002T) #{Z_Q},
[Gaussian law](fdip-0005) #{\mu_Q}, and
[retained marginal](fdip-000E) #{\mu_S} are derived data.
For the four [split components](fdip-001T),
#{S=A-BC^{-1}E}; the real orthogonal specialization has
#{E=B^\dagger}; the block #{B:Y\to X} is distinct from incidence.}

\p{An \newvocab{exact relabeling} to a primed background consists of
bijections #{p:V\to V'}, #{q:E\to E'} satisfying
##{\begin{aligned}
s'q&=ps,&h'q&=ph,\\
w'q&=w,&m'&=m,\qquad pR=R'.
\end{aligned}}
It is the all-positive specialization of a
\vocabk{signed endpoint arrow}{fdip-003B}. It preserves the attachment
of a loop even when its incidence is zero. The induced maps are
##{(Pf)(p(v))=f(v),\qquad (Ja)(q(e))=a(e).}
They preserve both counting pairings, the weighted edge pairing, and
by [isometry of reference volume](fdip-002L) the reference volume.
Allowing coherent endpoint reversals gives the larger signed relation;
it does not change what an exact directed relabeling means.}

\p{Evaluation at #{q(e)} gives
#{(d'Pf)(q(e))=f(h(e))-f(s(e))=(Jdf)(q(e))}.
Weight preservation gives #{W'J=JW}. Thus
[paired transport](fdip-003D) yields
##{L'=PLP^{-1},\qquad Q'=PQP^{-1}.}
Counting isometry gives #{P^{-1}=P^T}, #{J^{-1}=J^T} in the
coordinate bases, recovering #{D'=JDP^T} and #{W'=JWJ^T}.
The [normalized-law theorem](fdip-000H) carries the laws and moving
marginals. Identity, inverse and composition are the directed cases
of the proved signed-arrow laws, so existence of an exact relabeling
is an equivalence relation on backgrounds.}

\p{Two broader comparisons retain less data. The
[positive-scaling Laplacian relation](fdip-003H) forgets mass, state
and partition, and compares nonzero Laplacians by #{L'=cPLP^{-1}}
with #{c>0} and a vertex permutation. Its
[cycle trace ratio](fdip-000I) still separates two examples.
An arbitrary invertible real field map #{y=T\phi} instead changes
precision to #{T^{-T}QT^{-1}} and the counting pairing to
#{y^TT^{-T}T^{-1}z}. Its [reference Jacobian](fdip-002K) is
#{dy=|\det T|\,d\phi}. Such a map can transport the
[normalized law by Jacobian compensation](fdip-003M), while
[losing the reference pairing or retained projection](fdip-000Z).
It is not thereby an enriched background arrow.}

\p{The covariance-parameter Gaussian density in
\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical} agrees with
these conventions after the [inverse-precision theorem](fdip-000B)
identifies #{\Sigma=Q^{-1}}. Its change-of-variables argument does
not specify the endpoint, mass or retained-subset data.
Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.reindexLinearEquiv} and
\leanref{Matrix.reindexRingEquiv} support the coordinate reindexing
identities. The extra background requirements are those stated here.}
